/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*---------------------------- File string.dox -----------------------------*/
/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/** \addtogroup area_string */
/** \defgroup theory_string Theory of strings
 \ingroup area_string

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/string` realize the algorithms on
 strings of chapters 13 and 14 of the book (the approximate matching, the
 longest common subsequence, the alignments, the segmentations and the code
 of Huffman, all by dynamic programming or by a greedy choice) and, beyond
 the book, those of chapters 9, Searching Strings by Prefix, and 10,
 Searching Strings by Substring, of Ferragina's book and of the papers that
 the headers cite, i.e., the exact search of one pattern and of many,
 the edit distance by bit vectors, the dictionaries searched by prefix, the
 suffix arrays, trees and automata with their applications, and a few
 classical problems on palindromes, Lyndon words and parentheses. This page
 collects what the headers take for granted: the definitions and the
 notation shared by all of them (see \ref theory_string_notation), and, for
 each family of algorithms, the property on which its correctness rests and
 the argument behind its bound (from \ref theory_string_exact to
 \ref theory_string_segment). The pseudocode of each algorithm is instead on
 the page of its header, in the comment of its function, together with the
 section of the book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_string_notation Definitions and notation

 An <em>alphabet</em> \f$ \Sigma \f$ is a finite set of \f$ \sigma \f$
 symbols with a total order, the bytes of a `std::string_view` (\f$ \sigma
 \le 256 \f$) or the integers in \f$ [ 0 , K ) \f$ of a vector of
 `Symbols`, and a <em>string</em> \f$ T = T[ 0 .. n - 1 ] \f$ of length
 \f$ n = |T| \f$ is a sequence of \f$ n \f$ symbols, the places counting
 from 0 as in the code (the book and Ferragina's book count from 1, and
 the headers say where they translate). The string \f$ T[ i .. j ] \f$, for
 \f$ 0 \le i \le j < n \f$, is a <em>substring</em> of \f$ T \f$, a
 <em>prefix</em> if \f$ i = 0 \f$ and a <em>suffix</em> if \f$ j = n - 1
 \f$; the suffix that starts at \f$ i \f$ is called the suffix \f$ i \f$,
 \f$ T[ i .. ] \f$ for short, and the prefix of \f$ i \f$ symbols of the
 book is \f$ T( i ) = T[ 0 .. i - 1 ] \f$. A <em>subsequence</em> keeps
 some of the symbols in their order, not necessarily contiguous. Two
 strings are compared in the lexicographic order, a string that is a prefix
 of another being the smaller; the terminator \f$ \$ \f$ that some
 structures append is a symbol smaller than all the others, so that no
 suffix of \f$ T \$ \f$ is a prefix of another.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ T \f$, \f$ n \f$; \f$ P \f$, \f$ m \f$ (or \f$ p \f$)</td>
     <td>the text and its length; the pattern searched in it and its
         length, \f$ P_0 , \ldots , P_{k-1} \f$ for several patterns of
         \f$ M \f$ symbols in all</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathrm{Occ}( P , T ) \f$, \f$ \mathit{occ} \f$</td>
     <td>the places \f$ s \in [ 0 , n - m ] \f$ with
         \f$ T[ s .. s + m - 1 ] = P \f$, and their number</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathrm{lcp}( x , y ) \f$,
         \f$ \mathrm{lce}( i , j ) \f$</td>
     <td>the length of the longest common prefix of two strings, and that
         of the suffixes \f$ i \f$ and \f$ j \f$ of one text, their longest
         common extension</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{SA} \f$, \f$ \mathit{SA}^{-1} \f$,
         \f$ \mathit{LCP} \f$</td>
     <td>the suffix array, \f$ \mathit{SA}[ r ] \f$ the start of the
         \f$ r \f$-th smallest suffix; its inverse, the rank of each suffix;
         and \f$ \mathit{LCP}[ r ] = \mathrm{lcp}( T[ \mathit{SA}[ r ] .. ] ,
         T[ \mathit{SA}[ r + 1 ] .. ] ) \f$, \f$ n - 1 \f$ entries</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ F[ q ] \f$</td>
     <td>the failure function of \f$ P \f$, the length of the longest
         proper border of \f$ P[ 0 .. q - 1 ] \f$, a <em>border</em> being a
         string that is both a prefix and a suffix</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ D[ i ][ j ] \f$, \f$ \mathit{DP}[ i ][ j ] \f$</td>
     <td>the table of a dynamic programming on the prefixes of
         \f$ i \f$ symbols of the first string and of \f$ j \f$ of the
         second; \f$ \delta_{ij} \f$ is 0 (or \f$ +1 \f$ in a score) when
         the symbols \f$ i - 1 \f$ and \f$ j - 1 \f$ are equal</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ d \f$, \f$ k \f$</td>
     <td>the edit distance of two strings, and a bound on it, i.e., the
         errors (or the mismatches) allowed</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ w \f$</td>
     <td>the length of a machine word, 64 bits, by which the bit-parallel
         algorithms divide their work</td></tr>
 </table>

 The <em>edit distance</em> (of Levenshtein) of two strings is the least
 number of substitutions, insertions and deletions of single symbols that
 turn one into the other. A <em>period</em> of \f$ P \f$ is an integer
 \f$ q > 0 \f$ with \f$ P[ i ] = P[ i + q ] \f$ wherever both are defined;
 \f$ q \f$ is a period if and only if \f$ P[ 0 .. m - q - 1 ] \f$ is a
 border, and the smallest period is \f$ m - F[ m ] \f$.

 \section theory_string_exact The search of one pattern

 The problem is to find \f$ \mathrm{Occ}( P , T ) \f$. Trying every shift
 costs \f$ O( n m ) \f$ comparisons (`naiveMatching()` of
 `StringMatching.h`), and the algorithms differ in how much they learn from
 a comparison and how far they then move the pattern.

 <em>Lemma 1 (the failure function).</em> If \f$ P[ 0 .. q - 1 ] \f$
 matches the text up to the place \f$ j - 1 \f$ and
 \f$ P[ q ] \ne T[ j ] \f$, no occurrence starts at a place \f$ s \f$ with
 \f$ j - q < s < j - F[ q ] \f$, and the next candidate has
 \f$ P[ 0 .. F[ q ] - 1 ] \f$ already matched, i.e.,
 \f[
   q \gets F[ q ] \quad \text{and } T[ j ] \text{ compared again with }
   P[ q ] . \tag{1}
 \f]

 Indeed, an occurrence that starts at \f$ j - q + s \f$ with
 \f$ 0 < s < q - F[ q ] \f$ would make \f$ P[ 0 .. q - s - 1 ] \f$ a border
 of \f$ P[ 0 .. q - 1 ] \f$ longer than \f$ F[ q ] \f$. The algorithm of
 Knuth, Morris and Pratt (`kmp()`) applies (1) at each mismatch, and each
 comparison either advances \f$ j \f$, which happens at most \f$ n \f$
 times, or moves the start \f$ j - q \f$ of the pattern forward, which
 also happens at most \f$ n \f$ times; hence at most \f$ 2 n \f$
 comparisons, and the same argument on \f$ P \f$ against itself computes
 \f$ F \f$ in \f$ O( m ) \f$. The strong failure function of Knuth
 (`kmpStrong()`) skips a border whose next symbol is the one that just
 failed, so that a symbol of \f$ T \f$ is never compared twice with the
 same symbol of \f$ P \f$.

 The Z-function \f$ Z[ i ] = \mathrm{lcp}( S , S[ i .. ] ) \f$
 (`zFunction()`) rests on the same reuse: inside the box
 \f$ [ l , r ) \f$ that reaches farthest, \f$ S[ i .. r - 1 ] \f$ equals
 \f$ S[ i - l .. r - l - 1 ] \f$, and hence
 \f$ Z[ i ] \ge \min( Z[ i - l ] , r - i ) \f$; only the symbols past
 \f$ r \f$ are compared, each successful comparison moves \f$ r \f$
 forward, and each \f$ i \f$ fails at most once, which gives
 \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$. The bit-parallel Shift-Or (`shiftOr()`) keeps in one
 word \f$ D \f$ the prefixes of \f$ P \f$ that end at the current symbol,
 the bit \f$ i \f$ clear when \f$ P[ 0 .. i ] = T[ j - i .. j ] \f$, and
 updates them for all \f$ i \f$ at once by
 \f[
   D \gets ( D \ll 1 ) \vee B[ T[ j ] ] , \tag{2}
 \f]
 the bit \f$ i \f$ of \f$ B[ c ] \f$ clear when \f$ P[ i ] = c \f$, since
 \f$ P[ 0 .. i ] \f$ ends at \f$ j \f$ exactly when \f$ P[ 0 .. i - 1 ] \f$
 ends at \f$ j - 1 \f$ and \f$ P[ i ] = T[ j ] \f$; an occurrence ends at
 \f$ j \f$ when the bit \f$ m - 1 \f$ is clear, \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ word
 operations for \f$ m \le w \f$.

 The algorithm of Boyer and Moore (`boyerMoore()`) compares the window
 from right to left, and on a mismatch moves it by the larger of two safe
 shifts: the bad character rule aligns the symbol of \f$ T \f$ that failed
 with its last place in \f$ P \f$, and the strong good suffix rule aligns
 the suffix already matched with its previous occurrence in \f$ P \f$
 preceded by another symbol (or with the longest prefix of \f$ P \f$ that
 is its suffix). A shift is safe because any smaller one would put a
 different symbol under a symbol of \f$ T \f$ already read. When the
 symbols of \f$ T \f$ seldom occur in \f$ P \f$, the window moves by about
 \f$ m \f$ after a few comparisons, \f$ O( n / m ) \f$ comparisons at
 best; the worst case is \f$ O( n m ) \f$ for all the occurrences (each
 of the \f$ n - m + 1 \f$ windows of \f$ a^m \f$ in \f$ a^n \f$ is read
 whole). Horspool (`horspool()`) keeps only the bad character of the last
 symbol of the window, which is simpler and often faster on large
 alphabets.

 <em>Theorem 2 (the critical factorization).</em> Every pattern with
 \f$ m \ge 2 \f$ can be cut as \f$ P = u v \f$ with \f$ |u| \f$ smaller than
 the period of \f$ P \f$, so that the local period at the cut, the least
 \f$ q \f$ such that \f$ u \f$ and \f$ v \f$ agree with a shift of
 \f$ q \f$ around it, equals the period of \f$ P \f$; the cut is found
 from the two maximal suffixes of \f$ P \f$ for an order of the symbols and
 its reverse (Crochemore and Perrin, after Césari and Vincent).

 The Two-Way algorithm (`twoWay()`) compares \f$ v \f$ from left to right
 and then \f$ u \f$ from right to left: a mismatch in \f$ v \f$ at the
 place \f$ i \f$ moves the window by \f$ i - |u| + 1 \f$, each symbol of
 \f$ T \f$ read in \f$ v \f$ being left behind, and a mismatch in \f$ u \f$
 moves it by the period, the prefix of the period known to match being
 remembered when \f$ P \f$ is periodic. By Theorem 2 neither shift passes an
 occurrence; it makes at most \f$ 2 n \f$ comparisons in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$
 extra space, which is why the engineered `StringSearcher` of
 `StringSearch.h` falls back on it once its filters have compared more than
 \f$ 16 n + 4096 \f$ bytes.

 <table>
