 </table>

 \section theory_string_multi The search of many patterns

 The automaton of Aho and Corasick (`AhoCorasick.h`) generalizes Lemma 1 to
 \f$ k \f$ patterns: its states are the nodes of the trie of the patterns,
 each the string \f$ u \f$ spelt from the root, and the failure of \f$ u \f$
 is the state of the longest proper suffix of \f$ u \f$ that is in the
 trie.

 <em>Lemma 3 (the invariant of the automaton).</em> After the automaton has
 read \f$ T[ 0 .. j ] \f$, its state is the longest suffix of
 \f$ T[ 0 .. j ] \f$ that is a prefix of some pattern, and the patterns
 that end at \f$ j \f$ are the suffixes of that state that are patterns.

 Indeed, if \f$ u \f$ is the state after \f$ T[ 0 .. j - 1 ] \f$, every
 suffix of \f$ T[ 0 .. j ] \f$ that is a prefix of a pattern is \f$ x c \f$
 with \f$ c = T[ j ] \f$ and \f$ x \f$ a suffix of \f$ u \f$ in the trie,
 and the chain \f$ u , \mathit{fail}( u ) , \ldots \f$ lists those suffixes
 by decreasing length; the transition \f$ \delta( u , c ) \f$ takes the
 first of them that goes on with \f$ c \f$. With the transitions tabulated
 for every symbol a byte of \f$ T \f$ costs one lookup, and the output links
 (each to the nearest proper suffix of a state that is a whole pattern)
 list the occurrences that end at \f$ j \f$ at \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ each; hence
 \f$ O( n + \mathit{occ} ) \f$ time after a preparation of
 \f$ O( \sigma M ) \f$, the failures computed in breadth first order from
 \f$ \mathit{fail}( u c ) = \delta( \mathit{fail}( u ) , c ) \f$. The
 engineered `MultiSearcher` of `MultiSearch.h` puts filters in front of
 it, by the shuffles of the halves of the bytes or by a shift-or on super
 characters, which let through every occurrence and few other places; it
 checks the candidates directly and gives the text to the automaton when
 the checks exceed 2 a place, so that its worst case is that of the
 automaton.

 \section theory_string_dp Edit distance, alignments and subsequences

 The problems of chapter 13 of the book compare two strings, \f$ P \f$ of
 \f$ m \f$ symbols and \f$ T \f$ of \f$ n \f$, by a table on their
 prefixes. The edit distance of `editDistance()` of
 `ApproximateMatching.h` is
 \f[
   D[ i ][ j ] = \min\{ D[ i - 1 ][ j - 1 ] + \delta_{ij} ,
                        D[ i - 1 ][ j ] + 1 , D[ i ][ j - 1 ] + 1 \} ,
   \tag{3}
 \f]
 with \f$ D[ i ][ 0 ] = i \f$ and \f$ D[ 0 ][ j ] = j \f$, since the last
 column of an optimal sequence of edits aligns \f$ P[ i - 1 ] \f$ with
 \f$ T[ j - 1 ] \f$, or deletes one of the two, and what precedes it is an
 optimal sequence for the shorter prefixes; the table costs
 \f$ \Theta( m n ) \f$ time, and two rows suffice for the value
 (`stringMatchingSingleRow()`). With \f$ D[ 0 ][ j ] = 0 \f$ instead, an
 occurrence may start anywhere in \f$ T \f$, and \f$ D[ m ][ j ] \f$ is
 the least number of errors of an occurrence of \f$ P \f$ that ends at
 \f$ j \f$ (`stringMatching()`, the approximate matching of section 13.1,
 after Sellers). The same scheme with a maximum gives the longest common
 subsequence of `LongestCommonSubsequence.h` (section 13.7),
 \f[
   \mathit{DP}[ i ][ j ] = \left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
     \mathit{DP}[ i - 1 ][ j - 1 ] + 1 & P[ i - 1 ] = T[ j - 1 ] , \cr
     \max\{ \mathit{DP}[ i - 1 ][ j ] , \mathit{DP}[ i ][ j - 1 ] \} &
     \text{otherwise,}
   \end{array} \right. \tag{4}
 \f]
 since a common subsequence either uses both last symbols, when they are
 equal (and then some longest one does, by an exchange), or leaves out one
 of them; and the alignments of `Alignment.h` (exercises 13.7 to 13.9),
 whose score is the maximum of
 \f$ \mathit{DP}[ i - 1 ][ j - 1 ] + \delta_{ij} \f$ and of
 \f$ \mathit{DP}[ i - 1 ][ j ] - 2 \f$ and \f$ \mathit{DP}[ i ][ j - 1 ] - 2
 \f$, global with the borders \f$ -2 i \f$ and \f$ -2 j \f$ (Needleman and
 Wunsch), semiglobal with the borders 0 and the best of the last row and
 column, local with 0 as a fourth term of the maximum and the best entry
 (Smith and Waterman). A solution, not only its value, is read back from
 the table by following, from the last entry, a term that gave the
 maximum or the minimum, in \f$ O( m + n ) \f$ steps (`lcs()`, `diff()`,
 `globalAlignment()`).

 The relatives of `SubsequenceProblems.h` follow from (4). A shortest common
 supersequence of \f$ X \f$ and \f$ Y \f$ writes the items of a longest
 common subsequence once and the others in their order, hence its length
 \f$ |X| + |Y| - | \mathrm{LCS}( X , Y ) | \f$; the occurrences of \f$ P \f$
 as a subsequence of \f$ T \f$ add, at each symbol of \f$ T \f$ equal to
 \f$ P[ j - 1 ] \f$, the occurrences of \f$ P[ 0 .. j - 2 ] \f$ before it;
 and three strings need the table in three dimensions,
 \f$ \Theta( n m l ) \f$, since a longest common subsequence of two of them
 may leave out the items that the third shares. The problems on an
 unbounded number of strings are NP-hard (Maier; Mansfield for the
 shuffle).

 The engineered algorithms of `EditDistance.h` rest on two properties of
 (3).

 <em>Lemma 4 (the differences of the table).</em> Two adjacent entries of
 (3), in a row or in a column, differ by \f$ -1 \f$, 0 or \f$ +1 \f$, and
 along a diagonal the entries never decrease,
 \f[
   D[ i ][ j ] - D[ i - 1 ][ j ] \in \{ -1 , 0 , +1 \} , \qquad
   D[ i - 1 ][ j - 1 ] \le D[ i ][ j ] . \tag{5}
 \f]

 The first holds since an alignment of \f$ P( i - 1 ) \f$ with
 \f$ T( j ) \f$ becomes one of \f$ P( i ) \f$ by one more edit, and
 back, and likewise for \f$ T \f$; the second follows from the first,
 since each term of (3) is at least \f$ D[ i - 1 ][ j - 1 ] \f$, e.g.,
 \f$ D[ i - 1 ][ j ] + 1 \ge D[ i - 1 ][ j - 1 ] \f$ by a horizontal
 difference. By the
 first, a column is determined by its top entry and the vertical
 differences, two bit vectors \f$ P_v \f$ and \f$ M_v \f$ of \f$ m \f$
 bits, and Myers moves them to the next column by a constant number of
 operations on words, the carries of one addition propagating the
 horizontal differences down the column; hence
 \f$ O( n \lceil m / w \rceil ) \f$ time for the distance and for the
 search (`levenshtein()`, `approximateMatches()`), and the bit-parallel
 LCS of Allison and Dix in the same bound (`lcsLength()`). By the second
 part, a cell of value at most \f$ k \f$ has one of value at most \f$ k \f$
 on its diagonal in the column before; hence the last row of value at most
 \f$ k \f$ goes down by at most one row per column, and the search of
 \f$ k \f$ errors needs only the blocks down to the last one with a cell of
 value at most \f$ k + 1 \f$ at its bottom (the cut-off of Ukkonen),
 \f$ O( n k / w ) \f$ expected on random texts. The distance, finally,
 crosses only the diagonals \f$ i - j \in [ -d , d - ( n - m ) ] \f$, each
 step off the main diagonal costing 1, and doubling a guess \f$ t \f$ of
 \f$ d \f$ until the band of \f$ t \f$ gives a value at most \f$ t \f$
 costs \f$ O( n d / w ) \f$ blocks in all, a geometric sum.

 By the second part of (5) the furthest row \f$ F_h( k ) \f$ of the
 diagonal \f$ k = j - i \f$ that \f$ h \f$ edits reach determines the
 table: it is the row reached by sliding along the matches of the
 diagonal from the best of the three cells that one more edit reaches,
 \f[
   F_h( k ) = \mathit{slide}_k( \max\{ F_{h-1}( k ) + 1 ,
              F_{h-1}( k + 1 ) + 1 , F_{h-1}( k - 1 ) \} ) , \tag{6}
 \f]
 and the distance is the least \f$ d \f$ with \f$ F_d( n - m ) = m \f$
 (the diagonal transitions of Landau and Vishkin, the furthest reaching
 paths of Myers). The rounds \f$ h \le d \f$ handle \f$ O( d ) \f$
 diagonals each, and the slides of one diagonal never go back, so that the
 time is \f$ O( ( n + m ) d ) \f$ in the worst case,
 \f$ O( n + d^2 ) \f$ expected on random strings, and
 \f$ O( n + d^2 ) \f$ in the worst case when each slide is a query of
 longest common extension in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ (see
 \ref theory_string_suffix).

 <table>
 <caption>The computations of the edit distance and of the LCS</caption>
 <tr><th>Function</th><th>Time</th><th>Space</th><th>Remarks</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>editDistance()</tt>, <tt>lcs()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( m n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ \Theta( m n ) \f$</td>
     <td>the table of the book, a solution read back</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>stringMatchingSingleRow()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( m n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>two rows, the value alone</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>levenshtein()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( n \lceil m / w \rceil ) \f$, about
     \f$ O( n d / w ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( m / w + \sigma \lceil m / w
     \rceil ) \f$</td><td>the diagonals (6) for small \f$ d \f$, then the
     band of Ukkonen</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>approximateMatches()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( n \lceil m / w \rceil ) \f$, \f$ O( n k / w ) \f$
     expected</td><td>as above</td><td>the cut-off of Ukkonen</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>lcsLength()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( n \lceil m / w \rceil ) \f$</td><td>as above</td>
     <td>Allison and Dix</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_string_suffix Suffix arrays, trees and automata

 The suffixes of \f$ T \f$ hold all its substrings as their prefixes, and
 the three indexes of this section sort them, or merge their common
 prefixes, so that a pattern is searched in time that depends on \f$ m \f$
 rather than on \f$ n \f$.

 <em>Lemma 5 (the range of a pattern).</em> The suffixes prefixed by
 \f$ P \f$ are consecutive in \f$ \mathit{SA} \f$: they are the suffixes
 not smaller than \f$ P \f$ and smaller than \f$ P \# \f$, \f$ \# \f$ a
 symbol larger than all the others.

 Indeed, the strings prefixed by \f$ P \f$ are exactly those between
 \f$ P \f$ and \f$ P \# \f$ in the lexicographic order. Hence two binary
 searches give the range (`range()` of `SuffixArray.h`), each step a
 comparison of \f$ P \f$ with a suffix, \f$ O( m \log n ) \f$ time, and the
 occurrences are then listed in \f$ O( \mathit{occ} ) \f$. The search of
 Manber and Myers (`rangeLcp()`) keeps the lcp \f$ l \f$ and \f$ r \f$ of
 \f$ P \f$ with the suffixes at the two ends of the range, and stores for
 the middle of each range of the search its lcp with the two ends
 (\f$ \mathit{Llcp} \f$ and \f$ \mathit{Rlcp} \f$, \f$ 2 n \f$ integers);
 if, e.g., \f$ l \ge r \f$ and \f$ \mathit{Llcp} \f$ of the middle differs
 from \f$ l \f$, the side is decided with no comparison, and otherwise the
 comparison starts from \f$ \max( l , r ) \f$. That maximum never
 decreases, each equal symbol increases it and each step ends at the first
 different one; hence \f$ O( m + \log n ) \f$ comparisons.

 <em>Lemma 6 (Kasai et al.).</em> If the suffix \f$ i \f$ has lcp
 \f$ h > 0 \f$ with the suffix before it in \f$ \mathit{SA} \f$, then the
 suffix \f$ i + 1 \f$ has lcp at least \f$ h - 1 \f$ with the suffix before
 it in \f$ \mathit{SA} \f$:
 \f[
   \mathit{PLCP}[ i + 1 ] \ge \mathit{PLCP}[ i ] - 1 , \tag{7}
 \f]
 \f$ \mathit{PLCP}[ i ] \f$ being the lcp of the suffix \f$ i \f$ with its
 predecessor in \f$ \mathit{SA} \f$.

 In fact, if the suffix \f$ k \f$ precedes the suffix \f$ i \f$ and shares
 \f$ h > 0 \f$ symbols with it, the suffix \f$ k + 1 \f$ precedes the
 suffix \f$ i + 1 \f$ and shares \f$ h - 1 \f$ symbols with it, and so
 does every suffix between them in \f$ \mathit{SA} \f$. Computing the lcp
 in the order of the text from the value at \f$ i - 1 \f$ less one
 (`lcpArray()`, `lcpArrayPhi()` of `SuffixSorting.h`) therefore decreases
 \f$ h \f$ at most \f$ n \f$ times, and \f$ h \le n \f$; hence at most
 \f$ 2 n \f$ comparisons of symbols.

 The constructions of the suffix array differ in how they reuse the order
 of shorter strings. The prefix doubling sorts the suffixes by their
 prefixes of \f$ 2^{k+1} \f$ symbols as the pairs of the ranks of two
 prefixes of \f$ 2^k \f$ symbols, each round a radix sort in
 \f$ O( n ) \f$, and \f$ \lceil \log_2 n \rceil \f$ rounds suffice. The
 Skew algorithm sorts recursively the suffixes at the places
 \f$ i \bmod 3 \ne 0 \f$ by the names of their triples, sorts the others by
 a symbol and the rank of the next suffix, which is in the sample, and
 merges the two lists in \f$ O( n ) \f$ by comparing a pair or a triple
 that ends in the sample, so that
 \f$ T( n ) = T( 2 n / 3 ) + O( n ) = O( n ) \f$. The induced sorting of
 SA-IS rests on the following property.

 <em>Lemma 7 (the induced sorting).</em> Call the suffix \f$ i \f$ of type S
 if it is smaller than the suffix \f$ i + 1 \f$ and of type L otherwise,
 and LMS if it is of type S and the suffix \f$ i - 1 \f$ of type L. If the
 LMS-suffixes are placed at the ends of the buckets of their first symbols
 in their sorted order, a scan from left to right that puts each suffix
 \f$ i - 1 \f$ of type L at the head of its bucket, after reading the
 suffix \f$ i \f$, and a scan from right to left that puts each suffix
 \f$ i - 1 \f$ of type S at the tail of its bucket sort all the suffixes.

 Indeed, within the bucket of a symbol \f$ c \f$ the suffixes of type L
 come before those of type S, since the first are followed by a run of
 \f$ c \f$ and then a smaller symbol, the second by a larger one; and two
 suffixes \f$ i - 1 \f$ and \f$ j - 1 \f$ of type L in one bucket compare as
 the suffixes \f$ i \f$ and \f$ j \f$, which the first scan meets in
 their order, by induction on the scan, since each is smaller than the
 suffix before it. The second scan does the same for the type S, from the
 largest down. The LMS-substrings (from an LMS place to the next one) are
 sorted by the same two scans; if two of them are equal, their names form a
 string of at most \f$ n / 2 \f$ symbols, two LMS places never being
 adjacent, whose suffix array orders the LMS-suffixes; hence
 \f$ T( n ) \le T( n / 2 ) + O( n ) = O( n ) \f$.

 <table>
 <caption>The constructions of the suffix array</caption>
 <tr><th>Function</th><th>Time</th><th>Remarks</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>suffixArraySorting()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( n^2 \log n ) \f$, near \f$ O( n \log^2 n ) \f$ on random
     texts</td><td>a sort of comparisons, each as long as an lcp</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>suffixArrayDoubling()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( n \log n ) \f$</td><td>Manber and Myers, a radix sort of
     pairs of ranks a round</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>suffixArrayLarssonSadakane()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( n \log^2 n ) \f$ here, \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ with a
     ternary quicksort</td><td>only the groups still tied are
     sorted</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>suffixArraySkew()</tt></td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>the recursion on \f$ 2 n / 3 \f$ suffixes; optimal I/Os on
     disk</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>suffixArraySais()</tt>, <tt>suffixArray()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td><td>Lemma 7, the recursion on at most
     \f$ n / 2 \f$ suffixes; the engineered one in
     <tt>SuffixSorting.h</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>suffixArrayScan()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( ( n^2 / b ) \log b ) \f$ comparisons of suffixes</td>
     <td>blocks of \f$ b \f$ symbols, all its passes sequential</td></tr>
 </table>

 The suffix tree (`SuffixTree.h`) is the compact trie of the suffixes of
 \f$ T \$ \f$: it has \f$ n + 1 \f$ leaves, and since each internal node
 but the root has two children or more, at most \f$ n \f$ internal nodes;
 with each label kept as two places of \f$ T \f$ it takes \f$ O( n ) \f$
 space. An internal node spells a substring that occurs twice followed by
 different symbols, and the lowest common ancestor of the leaves \f$ i \f$
 and \f$ j \f$ spells their longest common prefix; hence
 \f$ \mathrm{lce}( i , j ) \f$ is the string depth of that ancestor, found
 in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ by the range minima of the depths along an Euler tour
 (Bender and Farach-Colton), and equivalently the minimum of
 \f$ \mathit{LCP} \f$ between the ranks of the two suffixes. McCreight's
 construction inserts the suffixes from the longest one, and starts the
 walk of the suffix \f$ i \f$ from the suffix link of the node of
 \f$ \mathit{head}_{i-1} \f$ (the longest prefix of the suffix
 \f$ i - 1 \f$ that occurs earlier), since \f$ \mathit{head}_{i-1} \f$
 without its first symbol is a prefix of \f$ \mathit{head}_i \f$. The
 rescan of a link not yet set jumps over whole edges, and the node depth
 drops by at most 2 at each link, so that its total is \f$ O( n ) \f$; the
 scan compares the symbols past the head, and since
 \f$ |\mathit{head}_i| \ge |\mathit{head}_{i-1}| - 1 \f$ its total is
 \f$ O( n ) \f$ too. With the children in a balanced tree, the time is
 \f$ O( n \log \sigma ) \f$. From \f$ \mathit{SA} \f$ and
 \f$ \mathit{LCP} \f$ instead the leaves are inserted in the order of
 \f$ \mathit{SA} \f$, each climbing from the previous leaf to the depth
 \f$ \mathit{LCP}[ r - 1 ] \f$; an edge climbed leaves the rightmost path
 for good, and the construction is linear.

 The suffix automaton (`SuffixAutomaton.h`) merges instead the substrings
 with the same set of end places,
 \f$ \mathrm{endpos}( u ) = \{ j : T[ j - |u| + 1 .. j ] = u \} \f$; two
 such sets are disjoint or nested, so that the classes form a tree, the
 suffix links, whose leaves are classes of prefixes of \f$ T \f$, and it has
 at most \f$ 2 n - 1 \f$ states and \f$ 3 n - 4 \f$ transitions (Blumer et
 al.). A class holds the suffixes of its longest member of lengths in
 \f$ ( \mathit{len}( \mathit{link}( v ) ) , \mathit{len}( v ) ] \f$, which
 gives the number of distinct nonempty substrings as
 \f$ \sum_{v} ( \mathit{len}( v ) - \mathit{len}( \mathit{link}( v ) ) ) \f$;
 in terms of the suffix array the same number is
 \f[
   \frac{n ( n + 1 )}{2} - \sum_{r} \mathit{LCP}[ r ] , \tag{8}
 \f]
 since the suffix \f$ \mathit{SA}[ r ] \f$ adds its prefixes but those it
 shares with the suffix before it (`distinctSubstrings()` of
 `SuffixApplications.h`). The other applications of that header follow
 from Lemma 5 and from the constant-time \f$ \mathrm{lce} \f$: a substring
 that occurs \f$ C \f$ times is a run of \f$ C - 1 \f$ entries of
 \f$ \mathit{LCP} \f$ that are at least its length; the occurrences of
 \f$ P \f$ with at most \f$ k \f$ mismatches are checked at each place by at
 most \f$ k + 1 \f$ extensions on the suffix array of \f$ T \# P \f$,
 \f$ O( n k ) \f$ (Landau and Vishkin); and the parsing of Ziv and Lempel
 copies at each place the longest prefix of the suffix that starts earlier,
 found on the suffix tree by walking down while the least leaf below the
 next node is smaller than the place. The engineered `LceIndex` of
 `LongestCommonExtension.h` answers the extensions by comparing the first
 64 symbols directly, which settles most of them on usual texts, and the
 others by range minima on blocks of 16 entries of \f$ \mathit{LCP} \f$, in
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ with two reads of memory in a row.

 \section theory_string_dict Dictionaries searched by prefix

 By Lemma 5 applied to a sorted array of \f$ n \f$ strings, the strings
 prefixed by \f$ P \f$ are those between the lexicographic positions of \f$ P
 \f$ and of \f$ P \# \f$ (`prefixRange()` of `PrefixSearch.h`), two binary
 searches of \f$ O( m \log n ) \f$ time and of \f$ O( ( m / B ) \log n ) \f$
 transfers of blocks of \f$ B \f$ symbols, a string far from the others being
 read at each step. The front coding of `FrontCoding.h` stores each string as
 the length \f$ \ell_i \f$ of its lcp with the string before and the rest of
 it, and decodes a string from the last one stored whole (an <em>anchor</em>);
 an anchor every \f$ b \f$ strings bounds the scan of a decoding, and the
 search becomes a binary search on the anchors and the scan of one bucket (a
 two-level scheme, whose first level is small enough to stay in memory). The
 locality-preserving front coding of Bender, Farach-Colton and Kuszmaul stores
 \f$ s_i \f$ whole when the anchor before it is more than \f$ c | s_i | \f$
 characters back: the decoding then reads at most \f$ ( c + 1 ) | s_i | \f$
 characters, and the anchors cost at most a factor \f$ 1 + 2 / ( c - 2 ) \f$ on
 the space of the front coding, since each anchor is paid by the \f$ c | s_i |
 \f$ characters coded before it, as Bender, Farach-Colton and Kuszmaul prove;
 the time of a decoding thus controls the space. The search by bins of Demaine,
 Jones and Pătrașcu (`BinnedSearch`) cuts the range of \f$ m \f$ sorted
 integers into \f$ m \f$ bins of equal width; a bin is at most as wide as the
 largest gap, and hence holds at most \f$ \Delta \f$ items, \f$ \Delta \f$ the
 ratio of the largest gap to the smallest, which bounds a search by \f$ O( \log
 \min( \Delta , m ) ) \f$.

 \section theory_string_combin Palindromes, Lyndon words and parentheses

 A palindrome reads the same in both directions. The algorithm of Manacher
 (`longestPalindromicSubstring()` of `Palindromes.h`) computes the radius
 of the longest palindrome about each of the \f$ 2 n - 1 \f$ centres, and
 keeps the palindrome found so far whose right end \f$ r \f$ is the
 largest: a centre inside it starts from the radius of its mirror, capped
 at \f$ r \f$, since the palindrome is symmetric, and compares only the
 symbols past \f$ r \f$; each successful comparison moves \f$ r \f$
 forward, so that the time is \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$. The longest palindromic
 subsequence is the longest common subsequence of \f$ s \f$ and of its
 reverse, by (4). The eertree (`Eertree`) has a node per distinct nonempty
 palindrome, and there are at most \f$ n \f$ of them, since a symbol added
 at the end creates at most one new palindrome, the longest palindromic
 suffix (a shorter one is a suffix of it, and hence its mirror, a prefix,
 occurred before); the lengths of the palindromic suffixes of a prefix form
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ arithmetic series, which gives the fewest palindromes
 of a factorization in \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ (`palindromicFactorization()`).

 <em>Theorem 8 (Chen, Fox and Lyndon).</em> A <em>Lyndon word</em> is a
 nonempty string strictly smaller than all its proper suffixes; every
 string has exactly one factorization
 \f[
   S = w_1 w_2 \cdots w_k , \qquad w_1 \ge w_2 \ge \cdots \ge w_k , \tag{9}
 \f]
 into Lyndon words.

 The algorithm of Duval (`lyndonFactorization()` of `Lyndon.h`) keeps the
 unfactored part as \f$ w^h w' \f$, \f$ w \f$ a Lyndon word and \f$ w' \f$ a
 proper prefix of it, and compares the next symbol with the one
 \f$ |w| \f$ places before: an equal one extends \f$ w' \f$, a larger one
 makes the whole part a Lyndon word, and a smaller one outputs the
 \f$ h \f$ copies of \f$ w \f$, which are factors of (9). Each comparison
 either advances the scan or comes with the output of factors at least as
 long as the symbols scanned again, hence \f$ O( n ) \f$ time; the least
 rotation of \f$ S \f$ starts where the factor of \f$ S S \f$ that covers
 the place \f$ n \f$ starts. The longest balanced subsequence of
 parentheses (`Parentheses.h`) matches each close with the nearest open
 still unmatched, which an exchange shows to lose no pair, and the longest
 balanced substring ending at a close starts after the open still unmatched
 below it on a stack, both in \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$. Two words are anagrams
 when their symbols sorted are equal, which gives a canonical signature
 for the hashing of `anagramGroups()` of `Anagrams.h`.

 \section theory_string_segment Segmentations, cuts and the code of Huffman

 A string \f$ s \f$ is a sequence of words of a dictionary if and only if
 some prefix \f$ s[ 0 .. j ) \f$ is and \f$ s[ j .. i ) \f$ is a word for
 \f$ i = n \f$ (exercise 13.13), which `wordBreak()` of `WordBreak.h`
 fills for every \f$ i \f$; the number of the segmentations adds, instead,
 the counts of the words that start at each place
 (`countSegmentations()` of `Segmentations.h`), and the readings of a
 string of digits as letters are its segmentations into the words 1 to 26
 (`countDecodings()`). The cuts of a string of length \f$ L \f$ at the
 places \f$ w_1 < \cdots < w_n \f$, each costing the length of the piece it
 splits, cost at least
 \f[
   D[ i ][ j ] = w_j - w_i + \min_{i < k < j} ( D[ i ][ k ] + D[ k ][ j ] )
   , \tag{10}
 \f]
 \f$ w_0 = 0 \f$ and \f$ w_{n+1} = L \f$, \f$ \Theta( n^3 ) \f$ in general.
 The cost \f$ w_j - w_i \f$ satisfies the quadrangle inequality as an
 equality and grows with the interval; hence (Knuth, Yao) the largest
 optimal split \f$ K[ i ][ j ] \f$ lies between \f$ K[ i ][ j - 1 ] \f$ and
 \f$ K[ i + 1 ][ j ] \f$, the candidates of the intervals of one length
 telescope to \f$ O( n ) \f$, and `StringBreaking.h` fills (10) in
 \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$.

 The code of Huffman (`huffman()` of `Huffman.h`, section 14.4 of the book)
 builds a prefix code of least expected length by merging, \f$ n - 1 \f$
 times, the two least frequent characters into a new one of their total
 frequency. Some optimal tree has the two least frequent characters as
 sibling leaves at the largest depth, since exchanging them with the leaves
 there does not increase the cost; and the cost of the tree with the merged
 character differs from that of the full tree by the sum of their
 frequencies, a constant; hence, by induction on the number of characters,
 the greedy choice is optimal, and with a heap it takes
 \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ time.

